{"text": "SP Diccustion Draft\n\nMarch 22, 1999\n\nDear State Extension Director:\n\nWe understand that many questions have been raised about Philip\nMorris U.S.A.\u2019s motives in supporting National 4-H Council\u2019s proposal to\ndevelop, implement and evaluate a nationwide youth smoking prevention\nprogram. I wanted to take this opportunity to tell you that our commitment\nto reducing youth smoking is very real.. We realize that others may not\nbelieve that, and only hope that they judge us over the long run for the\nimpact we make on this important youth issue.\n\nI want you to know that when we made our commitment to Council\nfor the two-year $4.3 million grant, it was made with an insistence on our\npart that Council would determine all program content and methods. We\nrealize that the program content may contain things, which we may not like\nor agree with. Our only interest is in putting resources behind efforts that\nare evaluated and measured for their impact on reducing youth smoking.\n\nCritics of the tobacco industry want to keep attacking us for the past,\ndespite the fact that Philip Morris continues to take aggressive actions to\naddress public concern about our products and practices. This sea change\nled to the creation of my job in April of 1998 and the dedication of\nsignificant financial and human resources towards the creation of a Youth\nSmoking Prevention department that I oversee. Our department of 12 is\nworking ona $100 million a year long-term, comprehensive effort that\nintegrates communication, education, community action and access\nprevention approaches. Our mission is to reduce youth smoking rates in the\nU.S. We are ensuring that our approaches are research-based and have\nbuilt-in measures of effectiveness because we want to see impact on this\nissue, not just input.\n\nIn addition, Philip Morris U.S.A.\u2019s marketing practices are\nsignificantly different as a result of agreements signed with state attorneys\ngeneral. Tobacco billboards are coming down; there will be no more\ncigarette branded gear and clothing and there will be advertising only in\npublications with overwhelming adult readership.\n\n166L06ZL02",
